---
name: sdlc-spec
description: Draft a feature spec (PRD and checks) from a requirements document (.pdf, .docx, .md or .txt). Type /sdlc-spec requirements.pdf.
argument-hint: requirements.pdf
disable-model-invocation: true
allowed-tools: Bash(./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc spec * --dry-run *) Bash(./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc implement * --dry-run *) Bash(git status *)
---

# /sdlc-spec $ARGUMENTS

Draft a specification from the requirements document `$ARGUMENTS`. **You are the agent; `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc` reads the document and checks the draft.**
You write only the spec: no code, no tests, and no other file. On Windows PowerShell or cmd, write `.\<%sdlcDir%>\sdlc.cmd` wherever this says `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc`.

1. No document given: ask for its path and stop. It must exist and be a `.pdf`, `.docx`, `.md` or `.txt` file.
<%#hasApiDocs%>
   The feature calls an API? The person brings its documentation in first, once, and commits it (step 2 wants a clean tree): `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc api import <file or link>` (an OpenAPI or Swagger file, a Postman collection, a guide). The dry run's prompt then lists it, and the draft must name only documented operations. You cannot import it for them from a dry run: say so and stop if it is missing.
<%/hasApiDocs%>
2. `git status --short` must print nothing: the tool wants a clean tree so that your draft is the only change. Otherwise ask the person to commit or stash first, and stop.
3. `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc spec $ARGUMENTS --dry-run --agent claude` (quote the path if it has spaces) creates nothing and starts no agent. It reads the document, then prints
   `would create: .ai/specs/FEAT-00N-<feature name>/` and `prompt: .ai/evidence/<run>/spec.prompt.md`. Read the prompt file to see what the document is about.
   If it describes several features, ask which one this is for.
4. Name the spec after the **feature**, never after the file: two to four lowercase words joined by hyphens, such as `customer-login` or `password-reset`, not `requirements`
   or `requirements-pdf`. Run the same dry run again with `--name <that-slug>` (and `--focus "<that part>"` for one feature of several): it now prints the real folder,
   `would create: .ai/specs/FEAT-00N-customer-login/`, and a prompt that points at it.
5. Create the folder it named and copy the source document into its `source/` subfolder (the CLI does that itself). Then **read the prompt file whole and do what it says**:
   it names the two files to write, their format, and the rules (every criterion cites the part of the document it comes from; gaps are listed as ASSUMPTIONS).
   One thing changes because you can talk to the person: where it says to write questions to a file and stop, ask the person here and wait.
6. Validate: `./<%sdlcDir%>/sdlc implement FEAT-00N --dry-run --agent claude` runs nothing and must report `spec ok`. If it lists problems, fix the files and run it again,
   up to three times; then stop and show what is still wrong.
7. Report: how many acceptance criteria and checks you wrote, every ASSUMPTION, and every open question. Say clearly that this is a **draft written by an AI**: the person
   compares it with the document, confirms or changes each ASSUMPTION, sets `Status: Accepted`, and only then runs `/sdlc-implement FEAT-00N`. Do not implement anything.
